From 3996fd47be615153170f55331fde79c232d1b8ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 17:56:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA/HIP: group independent rms_norm+mul launches into one
 kernel

Decode-time QK-norm issues two independent rms_norm+mul launches per layer
(Q and K) over disjoint tensors of the same ncols and eps. On a launch-bound
GPU each is its own dispatch of a ~1.6us kernel.

This adds a grouped rms_norm launch: up to 4 such pairs run as ONE kernel
whose grid concatenates their row ranges. A block routes itself to its
segment (x / mul / dst base pointer plus local row index - all integer work)
and then performs exactly the per-row thread geometry, reduction and output
expression of the individual rms_norm_f32<block_size, true> launch it
replaces, so results are byte-identical by construction.

Group detection runs in the eval loop before the existing fusions:

- Only pairs stock would run through the rms_norm+mul fused path are
  considered; the +add variant is left alone.
- Only the 2D case (ne02 == ne03 == 1 for the input and the mul operand) is
  grouped, where the stock channel/sample offsets are all zero.
- Later members are hoisted to the first member position only when their
  destination cannot touch memory any skipped-over node still reads or
  writes and their inputs cannot be written in between (checked against
  actual data ranges). Nodes already issued earlier in the pass - such as
  the K/V matvecs a grouped mmvq launch hoisted - are excluded from that
  scan: they have already completed at this launch point.
- Node order is untouched, so every allocation-sensitive upstream fusion
  decision stays identical to stock.

On Laguna-XS-2.1-Q4_K_M (RDNA4, gfx1201) this removes 38 kernel launches per
decoded token (38 of 40 layers group their Q/K norms) and improves decode
throughput by ~1.2 percent at byte-identical output.

GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_NORM_GROUP=1 disables the optimization.
